---
const steps = [
  {
    title: "Highlight",
    body: "Select a passage while you read. It stays marked in the app's own yellow.",
  },
  {
    title: "Annotate",
    body: "Attach a question to it. Quote and question travel together from here — the passage is the evidence, the question is what you actually want to know.",
    aside: "why does this contradict the section above?",
  },
  {
    title: "Stack",
    body: "Each pair queues, and survives the session — written to a file of its own, never into your repo. Read the whole document first, marking as you go.",
  },
  {
    title: "Send",
    body: "Push the stack to a Claude Code session inside the window: one message, every quote attached, file and line numbers included. The answer comes back typeset like the document, not painted into a terminal.",
  },
];
---

<section class="section section-alt" id="loop">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">what it is</span>
    <h2>Highlight → annotation → stack → send</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Terminal text is fine for code and miserable for prose. dreamd opens as
      its own window, lists the markdown in the repo you are sitting in, and
      renders it with real fonts, real spacing and highlighted code — built for
      a day inside tmux, Neovim and Claude Code, not a browser tab.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      {
        steps.map((step, i) => (
          <li>
            <span class="num" aria-hidden="true">
              {String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <div class="body">
              <h3>{step.title}</h3>
              <p>{step.body}</p>
              {step.aside && <p class="aside">{step.aside}</p>}
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
    <p class="payoff">
      Instead of retyping quotes into a chat box one at a time, you
      <mark>read normally</mark> and ship the batch at once.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  /* The old "what it is" section, absorbed: the section now opens by saying
     what the thing is and only then names the loop's four beats. */
  .lede {
    max-width: 36em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 2.6rem;
  }

  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
  }

  .steps li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr;
    gap: 0 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .body h3 {
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }

  .body p {
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 40em;
  }

  /* The annotation, drawn the way the app draws one: hanging off the line it
     belongs to, quieter than the thing it is about. */
  .aside {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .payoff {
    margin-top: 2.8rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 34em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .steps li {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.35rem;
    }
  }
</style>
